      "heading": "Release Notes and Frequently Asked Questions (Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel) > Understanding the data > Emergency department visit trends",
      "text": "**Continue exploring these data**\n\nNowcasts are built using advanced statistical models that incorporate historical data, current trends, and other relevant factors. This methodology allows us to provide timely estimates that reflect the most current understanding of hospitalization rates, even when complete data is not yet available.\n\n- **What Measures Are Taken to Ensure Accuracy?**\n  - We continuously validate our nowcasting models against actual hospitalization data as it becomes available. This process helps us refine our estimates and improve their accuracy over time. Additionally, we regularly review and update our models to incorporate the latest information and trends in hospitalization rates.\n- **Why Should I Trust This Data?**\n  - We understand that delayed data can raise concerns about accuracy. However, our commitment to transparency and rigorous statistical methods ensures that our nowcasts are reliable and provide a clearer picture of the current situation. We encourage users to view nowcasts as a tool to understand trends rather than definitive counts, and we will continue to provide updates as more complete data becomes available."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_faq#44",
      "doc_id": "resp_faq",
      "heading": "Release Notes and Frequently Asked Questions (Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel) > Release Notes > February 13, 2026 release",
      "text": "1\\. Change in Reporting for RESP-NET Hospitalization Data to Include \"Nowcasts\"\n\n**What**:\n\n- The data points for the 3 most recent weeks of data now represent estimates of RESP-NET hospitalization rates called \"nowcasts\". These are model-based projections of hospitalization rates, with the trend line representing the median estimate.\n\n**Why**:\n\n- These nowcasts account for the delayed reporting of recent hospitalizations, allowing us to more accurately estimate the true hospitalization rates from the partial data received thus far."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_faq#22",
      "doc_id": "resp_faq",
      "heading": "Release Notes and Frequently Asked Questions (Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel) > Understanding the data > What *Is* Reported",
      "text": "The Respiratory Illness Data Channel shares data collected from health systems when people seek care. The CDC collects data from these systems:\n\n- Positive clinical tests (from NREVSS \u2014 National Respiratory and Enteric Virus Surveillance System)\n- Emergency department visits for respiratory problems (from NSSP \u2014 National Syndromic Surveillance Program)\n- Hospitalizations for respiratory illness (from NHSN, National Hospital Safety Network, and RESPNET, Respiratory Virus Hospitalization Surveillance Network)\n- Deaths caused by respiratory illness (from NVSS \u2014 National Vital Statistics System)\n\nThese systems **only** collect data from people who have sought medical care. The Respiratory Illness Data Channel also shares data from these additional systems to better understand respiratory illness risk in communities:\n\n- Virus concentration in wastewater systems (from NWSS \u2013 National Wastewater Surveillance System)\n- Immunizations to protect against respiratory illness (from NIS \u2013 National Immunization Survey)"
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_faq#24",
      "doc_id": "resp_faq",
      "heading": "Release Notes and Frequently Asked Questions (Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel) > Understanding the data > What *Isn't* Reported",
      "text": "Even for the disease data we do have, reporting may be delayed, making it more difficult to anticipate trends in disease activity. Modeling is a way to use data and math to fill in missing information and make short-term predictions about disease.\n\nNowcasting is one modeling approach that allows us to adjust estimates for reporting delays. This adjustment gives decision-makers earlier information about potential disease activity so they can act faster. CDC's epidemic trend estimates apply nowcasting to give the most up to date information on whether diseases are increasing or decreasing."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_faq#63",
      "doc_id": "resp_faq",
      "heading": "Release Notes and Frequently Asked Questions (Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel) > Release Notes > October 4, 2024 release",
      "text": "- What: We utilize the ARI metric to categorize the level of respiratory illnesses as Minimal, Low, Moderate, High, or Very High in two places:\n  - 'Your community snapshot' on the main page of the Respiratory Illness Data Channel alongside other state and community data.\n  - Respiratory Virus Activity Levels, where we replaced the map based on influenza-like illness (ILI) with a map using the new ARI metric to capture the level of respiratory illness activity across the US. ILI data continues to be available on the ILINet State Activity Indicator Map.\n- Why:\n  - The ARI metric provides a broader picture than ILI for evaluating overall respiratory illness trends. Some respiratory illnesses do not have fever as a main symptom and are not captured consistently in measures using ILI, which uses fever as a criterion.\n  - The new summary metric characterizes overall ARI activity to monitor the combined effects of multiple respiratory illnesses and when used in combination with laboratory confirmed pathogen specific surveillance can help monitor trends in illness associated with respiratory pathogens."
